description_text:
CALL FOR SOLUTIONS
Commercial Maritime Test-Platform and Event-Support Services
Call Reference Number: W900KK-27-D-SSV2
Army Open Solicitation Reference: W9128Z-25-S-A002
Issuing Contracting Office: ACC-APG-Orlando
Solution Brief Due Date/Time: 6 NOV 26, 11:59 PM Eastern
Questions Due Date/Time: 2 NOV 26, 11:59 PM Eastern
Submission Method: Email
Contracting Point of Contact: deleon.j.kelly.civ@army.mil; kurt.l.kleinlein.civ@army.mil; Christopher.l.crouch.civ@army.mil;
1. NOTICE
This is a competitive Call for Solutions issued under the Army Open Solicitation.
The Government seeks commercially available maritime test-platform and event-support services to enable Government-directed research, development, test, and evaluation (RDT&E), training, and rehearsal events involving autonomous and uncrewed maritime systems.
The Government anticipates award of one commercial-services indefinite-delivery, indefinite-quantity (IDIQ) contract. The Government anticipates negotiating an initial task order with the selected awardee for a min of three-month period of performance beginning January 2027.
This Call for Solutions is intended to identify commercially available capabilities and determine which respondent(s) offer the most promising solution. Detailed pricing is not required at this stage. The Government may invite one or more respondents to provide oral presentations, demonstrations, site visits, additional technical information, and/or a detailed technical and price proposal for negotiations.
The Government may select one or more respondents for further consideration, establish a competitive range, negotiate final scope and terms, make one or more awards, or make no award. The Government is not obligated to reimburse respondents for costs associated with preparing or submitting a response.
2. MISSION NEED
The Government requires access to a commercially operated maritime reference platform and associated support services to conduct time-sensitive, Government-directed experimentation, integration, developmental test, training, and rehearsal activities involving autonomous and uncrewed systems.
The desired solution must provide a safe, configurable, event-driven at-sea platform capable of supporting activities beyond fixed shore infrastructure. The platform will support temporary integration of Government-furnished systems and equipment; launch and recovery; deck handling; onboard power; communications; instrumentation; data collection; and other maritime support activities required for scheduled Government-directed events.
The Government seeks a commercially available solution that can achieve operational readiness in time to support an anticipated initial task order beginning in January 2027.
3. ANTICIPATED CONTRACT STRUCTURE
3.1 IDIQ Contract
The Government anticipates awarding one commercial-services IDIQ contract(s) with the following parameters:
Ordering Period: 1 JAN 27 – 30 DEC 27
Guaranteed Minimum: $500,000
Maximum Contract Value/Ceiling: $49,999,999
Anticipated Contract Type: Firm Fixed Price
Place of Performance: Government-designated pier-side locations, U.S. and international waters, approved maritime ranges, and contractor facilities as required.
Task-Order Scope: Commercial vessel availability, marine operations, temporary mission configuration, event support, uncrewed-system support, logistics, and related services.
3.2 Initial Task Order
The Government anticipates negotiating and awarding an initial task order concurrently with, or shortly after, IDIQ award.
Anticipated Initial Task-Order Period of Performance:
1 JAN 27 through 31 MAR 27 , subject to final solicitation terms and award.
The initial task order is expected to include:
Mobilization and vessel readiness validation;
Vessel availability and marine operations;
Temporary and reversible mission configuration;
Support for Government-furnished autonomous and uncrewed systems;
Pier-side and underway event support;
Launch and recovery, deck handling, staging, storage, and power support;
Readiness, operational-status, safety, and performance reporting; and
Demobilization and restoration, if applicable.
4. COMMERCIAL SOLUTION OBJECTIVES
The Government seeks commercially available solutions that provide the following outcomes:
4.1 Commercial Maritime Platform Availability
A commercially operated vessel or maritime platform available to support scheduled Government-directed events.
4.2 Safe Marine Operations
Qualified crew, vessel management, maintenance, logistics, and marine-support services necessary for safe pier-side and underway operations.
4.3 Temporary Mission Configuration
A commercially supportable approach to temporarily configure the vessel to accommodate Government-furnished systems, payloads, sensors, and support equipment. All modifications must be temporary and reversible unless otherwise authorized by the Government.
4.4 Uncrewed-System Support
Capability to support Government-led experimentation involving uncrewed surface, subsea, and other autonomous systems.
4.5 Event Execution Support
Support for Government-directed event planning, readiness, integration, instrumentation, data collection, test reconstruction, reporting, and after-action activities.
4.6 Schedule Reliability
A credible approach to achieving readiness for an anticipated January 2027 start and sustaining availability throughout the initial three-month task order.
4.7 Commercially Viable Terms
A commercial operating model that supports predictable vessel availability, event execution, and negotiation of reasonable contract terms.
5. DESIRED VESSEL AND SUPPORT CHARACTERISTICS
The Government desires a commercially operated vessel or platform with capabilities generally consistent with the following characteristics. Respondents may propose alternate commercial configurations that meet the Government’s functional needs and shall clearly identify any deviations.
Approximately 78-100 meters in length;
Approximately 15 meters in beam;
Approximately 5 meters in draft;
Dynamic Positioning Class 2 capability or equivalent station-keeping capability;
Global maritime operating range;
Configurable deck space and suitable mission areas;
Sufficient onboard power (230V 3-phase minimum) to support embarked Government systems;
Ship-to-shore communications capability;
Capability to support launch and recovery operations; (A-Frame / Knuckleboom Crane with articulated docking head)
Capability for deck handling, staging, and storage within vessel operating limits;
Ability to provide available power interfaces for Government-furnished systems;
Ability to support temporary instrumentation, data-collection, and communications equipment (compatible transducer pole); and
Suitability for Government-directed pier-side and underway RDT&E events.
Berthing to support minimum 16 personnel, not including ship’s crew
The Government will consider alternate vessel sizes, configurations, and capabilities where the respondent demonstrates that the proposed commercial solution satisfies the Government’s functional requirement.
6. SCOPE OF REQUESTED COMMERCIAL SERVICES
6.1 Vessel Availability and Marine Operations
The contractor shall provide a commercially operated vessel, qualified crew, management, maintenance, logistics, and vessel-support services necessary to safely support Government-directed RDT&E, training, and rehearsal events.
6.2 Temporary Mission Configuration
The contractor shall provide commercially supportable temporary and reversible vessel configuration, integration, and restoration services necessary to support embarked Government-furnished systems and event-specific activities.
Permanent construction, permanent alteration of real property, and permanent infrastructure build-out are outside the scope of this Call for Solutions.
6.3 Uncrewed-System Event Support
The contractor shall support Government-led experimentation involving uncrewed surface vehicles, uncrewed underwater vehicles, remotely operated vehicles, and other autonomous systems. Support may include:
Launch and recovery;
Station-keeping and maneuvering;
Deck handling;
Staging and storage;
Available power support;
Integration support;
Event logistics; and
Reporting as directed by the Government.
6.4 Event Support and Data Collection
The contractor shall provide commercial support for Government-directed event planning and execution, including vessel readiness, maritime logistics, instrumentation, data collection, test reconstruction, and after-action documentation.
6.5 Reporting
The contractor shall provide the following, at a minimum:
Vessel Readiness Confirmation before the start of performance;
Daily Operational Status updates when underway, as directed;
Immediate notification of conditions affecting vessel readiness, safety, or scheduled-event support;
Monthly cost, schedule, and status reports;
Trip or travel reports, when required;
Event-execution logs, as directed; and
An end-of-period operational and experimentation-support summary.
7. EXCLUSIONS
The following are outside the scope of this Call for Solutions:
Development of a new vessel or vessel class;
Production, fielding, or fleet sustainment of Government capabilities;
Operational mission execution;
Maritime security operations;
Force augmentation;
Enterprise advisory and assistance services;
Enterprise information technology consulting or enduring IT operations;
Permanent construction or permanent infrastructure build-out;
Inherently governmental functions; and
Personal services.
The contractor shall provide and supervise its own employees and shall remain responsible for the method and means of performance, subject to Government direction concerning required outcomes, event schedules, safety constraints, and acceptance criteria.
8. GOVERNMENT-FURNISHED PROPERTY AND INFORMATION
The Government anticipates providing autonomous and uncrewed platforms, equipment, information, and other property as Government-furnished property or information, as identified in the resulting solicitation, contract, or task order.
The contractor shall be responsible for stewardship, accountability, care, and authorized use of Government-furnished property in accordance with the resulting contract.
Respondents shall identify any Government-furnished equipment, property, information, facilities, access, approvals, or decisions necessary to execute their proposed solution.
9. SECURITY AND INFORMATION HANDLING
The resulting contract and task order will include applicable security, information-handling, export-control, operational-security, cybersecurity, Government-property, and personnel requirements.
The Government will provide applicable security requirements, including any DD Form 254, through the authorized solicitation and contract process.
Respondents shall not submit classified information in response to this Call for Solutions. Respondents may identify classified capabilities at a high level and coordinate separately with the Contracting Officer for authorized follow-on engagement.
10. SOLUTION-BRIEF INSTRUCTIONS
Respondents shall submit a solution brief of no more than 10 pages , excluding commercial literature, resumes, and appendices.
Detailed pricing is not required in response to this Call for Solutions. The Government is seeking sufficient information to assess commercial availability, technical capability, operational feasibility, relevant experience, schedule, and suitability for negotiations.
Each solution brief shall include the following:
10.1 Company Information
Legal business name;
Unique Entity Identifier and CAGE code, if available;
Business size;
Headquarters location;
Primary point of contact; and
Email address and telephone number.
10.2 Commercial Solution Description
Description of the commercially available vessel, services, equipment, operating practices, and support capabilities proposed;
Description of the solution’s commercial applications or prior commercial use;
Identification of any Government-unique adaptation, modification, integration, or development proposed; and
Identification of major commercial terms, assumptions, or constraints affecting vessel availability or performance.
10.3 Technical and Operational Capability
Vessel characteristics and operating limitations;
Dynamic positioning or station-keeping capability;
Deck space, mission areas, crane or handling capability, storage, and payload support;
Onboard power and communications capability
Launch-and-recovery capability;
Ability to support Government-furnished autonomous and uncrewed systems;
Approach to temporary and reversible mission configuration;
Approach to vessel readiness, safe operations, and restoration; and
Approach to support Government-directed RDT&E events.
10.4 Mobilization and Availability
Earliest date the proposed vessel and support personnel can be available;
Mobilization timeline;
Readiness plan for a planned January 2027 start;
Availability throughout the anticipated initial three-month task-order period;
Planned maintenance periods or competing commitments;
Key schedule risks; and
Recommended Government actions or decisions needed to preserve schedule.
10.5 Relevant Experience
Provide up to three examples of relevant prior or current efforts involving commercial maritime, offshore, autonomous-system, uncrewed-system, test-and-evaluation, or comparable event-support services.
For each example, provide:
Customer type;
Period of performance;
General scope;
Vessel or platform used;
Services performed;
Relevance to this requirement; and
Outcome or performance result.
10.6 Management, Safety, and Risk
Proposed management approach;
Proposed single point of contact;
Marine safety approach;
Quality-assurance approach;
Environmental-compliance approach;
Operational-risk-management approach;
Key assumptions and dependencies; and
Principal technical, schedule, safety, commercial, and Government-dependency risks.
10.7 Commercial Terms
Respondents shall identify standard commercial terms that may materially affect negotiation or performance, including:
Minimum charter or commitment periods;
Cancellation terms;
Mobilization and demobilization requirements;
Port and fuel assumptions;
Insurance requirements;
Liability limitations;
Third-party or subcontractor dependencies; and
Other commercial conditions relevant to availability or performance.
Respondents may provide rough-order-of-magnitude pricing ranges, commercial rate-card information, or commercial terms as an optional appendix. Detailed pricing is not required at this stage.
10.8 Security and Compliance Acknowledgment
Respondents shall acknowledge their ability to comply with applicable Government security, citizenship, information-handling, operational-security, export-control, Government-property, safety, and environmental requirements, as established in the eventual solicitation, contract, or task order.
11. EVALUATION AND DOWNSELECT
The Government will evaluate solution briefs to identify the respondent or respondents most likely to provide a commercially available, technically capable, schedule-feasible, and operationally suitable solution.
The Government intends to use the following evaluation factors, listed in descending order of importance:
Factor 1: Commercial Availability and Suitability
The extent to which the proposed vessel, equipment, and services are commercially available and can meet the Government’s requirement without substantial Government-unique development.
Factor 2: Technical and Operational Capability
The extent to which the respondent demonstrates the ability to provide a safe, suitable maritime platform and associated support services for temporary mission configuration, uncrewed-system support, launch and recovery, deck handling, power, communications, vessel operations, and Government-directed event execution.
Factor 3: Mobilization and Schedule Feasibility
The credibility of the respondent’s plan to achieve readiness for an anticipated January 2027 start and sustain support during the anticipated initial three-month task order.
Factor 4: Relevant Experience and Performance
The relevance and quality of the respondent’s demonstrated experience supporting comparable maritime, offshore, autonomous-system, uncrewed-system, or test-event activities.
Factor 5: Risk and Commercial Terms
The completeness and reasonableness of the respondent’s identified operational, schedule, safety, commercial, and Government-dependency risks and proposed mitigations.
Pricing is not an evaluation factor at the Call for Solutions stage. The Government will evaluate detailed price, price reasonableness, and final contract terms during negotiations with the selected respondent or respondents.
12. DOWNSELECT AND NEGOTIATION PROCESS
Following evaluation of solution briefs, the Government may:
Select one or more respondents for further consideration;
Request oral presentations, technical demonstrations, site visits, or additional information;
Down-select to a subset of solutions;
Request detailed technical and price proposals from selected respondent(s);
Conduct negotiations concerning technical requirements, vessel configuration, schedule, commercial terms, contract terms, and price;
Request proposal revisions;
Award one or more IDIQ contracts and an initial task order; or
Make no award.
The Government anticipates negotiating the initial task order with the selected IDIQ awardee. The negotiated proposal package for the initial task order may include a final technical approach, vessel-availability commitment, schedule, pricing, commercial terms, risk register, staffing/mobilization plan, and required supporting documentation.
13. ANTICIPATED INITIAL TASK-ORDER PRICING STRUCTURE
Detailed pricing will be requested only from selected respondent(s) during the negotiation phase. The Government anticipates using a commercial, event-based pricing structure, which may include the following contract line item groups:
CLIN Group
Description
Unit
0001
Mobilization and readiness validation
Lump Sum
0002
Vessel availability — pier-side
Day
0003
Vessel availability — underway
Day
0004
Temporary mission configuration
Lump Sum or Fixed Unit
0005
Uncrewed-system launch/recovery and deck support
Event Day
0006
Specialized equipment, third-party support, or authorized other direct costs
Fixed Unit / Authorized Cost
0007
Demobilization and restoration
Lump Sum
The Government may revise the final pricing structure based on the selected commercial solution and negotiated task-order scope.
14. SUBMISSION INSTRUCTIONS
Submit solution briefs no later than 6 NOV 26 by 11:59 PM Eastern to deleon.j.kelly.civ@army.mil; kurt.l.kleinlein.civ@army.mil; Christopher.l.crouch.civ@army.mil;
Questions shall be submitted no later than 2 NOV 26 at 11:59 PM Eastern to deleon.j.kelly.civ@army.mil; kurt.l.kleinlein.civ@army.mil; Christopher.l.crouch.civ@army.mil;
The Government may post consolidated responses, amendments, or additional instructions through the designated Army Open Solicitation channel on SAM.gov.
15. GOVERNMENT RESERVATION OF RIGHTS
The Government reserves the right to:
Amend, suspend, or cancel this Call for Solutions;
Seek additional information from any respondent;
Conduct oral presentations, demonstrations, site visits, or other exchanges;
Negotiate with one or more respondents;
Award one or more contracts or task orders;
Award without further exchanges;
Make no award; and
Reject any or all solution briefs.
Submission of a solution brief does not constitute an award commitment by the Government.

Description:
The Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) seeks to retain an independent third-party Commissioning Agent to perform comprehensive commissioning services for the subject project.
This project will upgrade and replace all 21 elevators and elevator components as necessary on the main Cincinnati campus as well as the Ft. Thomas campus. Asbestos and lead-based paint abatement is part of this project (if necessary).
Selection Criteria:
(1) Professional qualifications necessary for satisfactory performance of required services;
(2) Specialized experience and technical competence in the type of work required, including, where appropriate, experience in energy conservation, pollution prevention, waste reduction, and the use of recovered materials;
(3) Capacity to accomplish the work in the required time;
(4) Past performance on contracts with Government agencies and private industry in terms of cost control, quality of work, and compliance with performance schedules;
(5) Location in the general geographical area of the project and knowledge of the locality of the project; provided, that application of this criterion leaves an appropriate number of qualified firms, given the nature and size of the project;
(6) The extent to which potential contractors identify and commit to the use of service-disabled veteran owned small businesses, veteran-owned small businesses, and other types of small businesses as subcontractors; and
Submission Info:
Please submit your SF 330s to ronald.heidemann @va.gov by the closing date/time of this announcement. No physical copies will be accepted. Please include your firm s POC and UEI in the email body.
***End of Presolicitation Notice**
